A||N||14||kornholio||replacing 02 sensor on V6||||5||
Z||000000||kornholio||12-12-1999||07:57 PM||||I have a V6.  Do I need to get at the sensor from under the car?  I haven't taken a look at it yet, so I don't know if I can reach down from the top and change it.  How exactly can you torque the O2 sensor?  The wire on the sensor is not removeable.  Is it really important to torque it right?  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mona||12-12-1999||08:23 PM||||Yes you can reach the O2 sensor from the top <BR>& the pigtail has a connection that plugs into a wiring harness along the fire wall<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000002||Gokart||12-12-1999||08:52 PM||||I found it much easier to get it from the bottom with the back tires on ramps. Remember to take it out right after it's been running(unfortunatly)or you may break it off. Coat the threads with anti-seize as well.||||reg||2
Z||000003||Sage||12-12-1999||09:02 PM||||I have found it easiest to get to from the top.  If for no other reason, avoiding laying on your back on the ground!  There is a special socket made to remove and install o2 sensors, it is called an o2 sensor socket!  Most good parts houses should carry it, but it is not cheap.  It looks like a regular deep well socket but it has a slice out of it on one or both sides.(have seen both kinds)This is not a "have to have to do the job tool" but it makes the job so much easier.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||12-12-1999||09:20 PM||||Can someone tell me what the difference is between an AC Delco O2 sensor and one from a discount store?  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000005||2birds||12-12-1999||09:44 PM||||About 20 bucks... I paid 45 bucks for an AC Delco unit from a dealer, Western Auto, wanted around $25 for a Bosch (back in '96).  I'm nervous about aftermarket parts, even though I swear by Bosch platinum plugs in my Fiero.  Had an aftermarket TPS and fuel pump relay fail almost immediately after I bought them.||||reg||6
Z||000006||TOM||12-13-1999||12:08 AM||||To remove the O2 sensor from a fiero you dont need that special socket just a wrench. I think it is 22mm||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||12-14-1999||09:10 PM||||Tom's right. I reached mine from the top and used an open end wrench.  It helps if the head pipe is warm.  They come with the anti-sieze already on them, too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||kornholio||12-14-1999||09:49 PM||||Is it important to torque the O2 sensor?  Or should I just tighten it enough that it won't vibrate loose?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Gokart||12-14-1999||09:55 PM||||Didn't torque mine when I put it in last spring and she's runnin' fine and no codes. I just stopped when I could tell it was in far enough and didn't use too much force. Kind of like a spark plug, you'll know when you're there! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||kornholio||12-14-1999||10:01 PM||||OK, I think this will be my last two O2 sensor questions...How many of you, after changing the O2 sensor, got better gas mileage?  How much of an improvement?  Thanks for all the replys!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gokart||12-15-1999||07:11 AM||||I didn't really get to notice when I replaced mine cause the code to change it popped up a week after I got the car. Haven't gotten the code since though and I'm thankful for that!||||reg||2
Z||000012||William Federle||12-15-1999||08:30 AM||||I stopped getting code 13 when I replaced mine (in my 4 cyl) and I think it was the<BR>reason my MPG's went up by 1 or 2. <P>I squirted some Gunk liquid wrench on the<BR>base of the sensor (with the engine cold)<BR>and let it soak for about 15 minutes and <BR>it came out easy. I think it may not have<BR>been very tight to begin with though.<P>The new O2 sensor will smoke quite a lot<BR>for the first few minutes that the engine is<BR>running with it (if it works like mine) but<BR>then the smoke will stop.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-15-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||2birds||12-15-1999||06:59 PM||||I changed mine because the muffler shop (a well known national chain that shall remain nameless) refused to honor the warranty on the replacement catalytic converter unless I replaced the O2 sensor.  Get this.  Since I paid them for parts and labor on the cat job, they offered to replace the O2 sensor for the cost of the part.  FREE LABOR?  I think not.  Their price was $85 for a Bosch sensor (cost $25 at Western Auto).  Obviously I did it myself... Took about 5 minutes.  Certainly easier than the forward bank of spark plugs.||||reg||1
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||12-16-1999||01:18 PM||||When I replaced the O2 sensor on my wife's 87 four cyl I went aftermarket (I think it was a Borg-Warner)just so I could get through the smog. The day before I went to get a AC/Delco O2 the Code 13 came on. I looked back there and the point where the wire went into the sensor had melted from just the heat of everyday driving.<P>AC/Delco from now on.||||reg||1
